1
00:00:00,556 --> 00:00:04,026
A cipher is only strong
until someone attacks it.

2
00:00:04,436 --> 00:00:07,826
Cryptanalysis is the
art of breaking ciphers.

3
00:00:09,456 --> 00:00:11,106
Ciphertext-only attack.

4
00:00:11,716 --> 00:00:14,066
The attacker has only ciphertext.

5
00:00:14,576 --> 00:00:15,586
Most common.

6
00:00:16,191 --> 00:00:18,641
Frequency analysis is the tool.

7
00:00:20,322 --> 00:00:22,012
Known-plaintext attack.

8
00:00:22,572 --> 00:00:25,562
The attacker has some
plaintext-ciphertext pairs.

9
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:28,432
Known file headers give these away.

10
00:00:30,089 --> 00:00:31,919
Chosen-plaintext attack.

11
00:00:32,479 --> 00:00:35,889
The attacker picks plaintexts
and gets ciphertexts.

12
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:38,674
It breaks many classical ciphers.

13
00:00:40,322 --> 00:00:42,172
Chosen-ciphertext attack.

14
00:00:42,752 --> 00:00:46,022
The attacker picks ciphertexts
and gets plaintexts.

15
00:00:46,647 --> 00:00:48,957
Padding-oracle attacks work this way.

16
00:00:50,589 --> 00:00:52,819
Brute-force, or exhaustive search.

17
00:00:53,404 --> 00:00:55,334
Try every key in the key space.

18
00:00:55,879 --> 00:00:58,709
A large key space makes this infeasible.

19
00:01:00,289 --> 00:01:01,899
Mathematical attacks.

20
00:01:02,444 --> 00:01:07,974
Factoring, discrete log, structural
attacks on RSA and Diffie-Hellman.

21
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:11,006
Kerckhoffs's principle.

22
00:01:11,516 --> 00:01:15,206
Security must depend only
on the secrecy of the key,

23
00:01:15,656 --> 00:01:18,066
never on the secrecy of the algorithm.

24
00:01:19,856 --> 00:01:20,966
Shannon's properties.

25
00:01:21,721 --> 00:01:26,031
Confusion: complex relation
between key and ciphertext.

26
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:27,451
Diffusion:

27
00:01:27,941 --> 00:01:31,771
each plaintext digit influences
many ciphertext digits.

28
00:01:32,371 --> 00:01:34,681
Substitution gives confusion.

29
00:01:35,171 --> 00:01:37,381
Permutation gives diffusion.

30
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:40,959
Six attacks, two defenses.

31
00:01:41,739 --> 00:01:43,909
Large keys defeat brute force.

32
00:01:44,509 --> 00:01:47,859
Kerckhoffs and Shannon
define good cipher design.

